# The words of a hash go round a loop and come round out of `| 0`; what enters is read off a list of numbers, a view of unknown class, a pattern: it may be a double.
# In front of the typed build each word was asked for an int32. The state of MD5, SHA-1 and SHA-256 is kept as `>>> 0`: half of all words lie over 2 ** 31: one such word and all 64 turns ran in the closed copy, operator by operator through stubs.
# The word is asked for an integer from -2 ** 31 to 2 ** 32 - 1: bit operators take its low 32 bits, sums are exact in 64 bits, and with no turn at all the word itself is handed back. A fraction, -0, NaN, 2 ** 32 fail the test as before.
# compared: a word that the head compares stays asked for an int32: the head's relation wants one.
# -- source: a-word-that-comes-round-as-an-int32-may-enter-as-any-integer-of-32-bits-signed-or-not.ts
